      <cac:FrameworkAgreement>
        <cbc:MaximumOperatorQuantity>1</cbc:MaximumOperatorQuantity>
        <cbc:Justification languageID="ENG">The extended FwC duration is necessary for the following reasons:
•	Experience in predecessor contracts has shown that the volume of activities and the related workforces necessary to be supplied by the contractor need to be planned over a longer contractual period to ensure a longer return on investment period from both sides.
•	Phase-in/phase-over activities at the start of the contract are followed by a long ramp-up period (1-2 years) to come to the expected quality of delivery. This period covers not only the contractual phase in but also the ramp up to the level of quality expected.
•	To bring its service delivery to the expected quality, the contractor will have to make investments not only in human resources (recruitment, training, getting knowledgeable about the EC setting), but equally in technological integrations. Those investments will have a higher output for the EC, if the contractor is investing in more sustainable integrations as the period of usage will be longer.  
•	Transformational activities in the DWP domain, expected to be performed and taken on during the duration of the contract have a timeline for several years and a disruption in the contractual setup during the transformation would be disruptive.
•	Ramp-down activities from the contractor and the end of transformational activities will start at least one year prior to the contract end-date to ensure a stable situation at the contract transition at the end of the contract.
Taking these constraints into account (3 years for a full phase-in, phase-out, ramp-up and ramp-down), the period of stable service delivery has to be maximized as much as possible and this would not be possible with a standard 4 years contractual period.
In making the reconduction of the contract dependent of the contractual performance and the evolution of the underlying DWP solutions and flexible with a minimum and maximum period, the contract will cater for the necessary robustness for solid service delivery.</cbc:Justification>
      </cac:FrameworkAgreement>
